[[:db/retract 17592186122798 :answer-field/choices 17592186122800] [:db/retract 17592186122798 :answer-field/choices 17592186122801] [:db/retract 17592186122798 :answer-field/choices 17592186122802] [:db/retract 17592186122798 :answer-field/choices 17592186122803] [:db/retract 17592186122798 :answer-field/choices 17592186122799] {:db/id 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2504620-1607-5645-b4d2-e55a27da4e3f" "question-11bccf6f-4ced-5e74-80c9-222565fe2e6b" "question-8bdc5f30-6e9d-55a3-aacc-08dbc13064bd" "question-8052460c-148b-5893-b35a-1e1c771c0935"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-004a7cc4-06c2-5abf-9f5d-0dfb86998289" "question-e78c82da-b48f-5dab-aeb6-fcde924129f1" "question-a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" "question-49397200-f106-5784-934d-a685f0e1e2bc" "question-8fb41ea9-db84-5bfb-a8a6-5486c5782ac8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124658 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-57601ebb-8cf3-5513-9b66-f85999e2eebc" "question-22939b6a-86a1-5a24-b491-90aeb5c6c381" "question-e240afca-8d54-5fc1-a704-b3948ecfdb13"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124221 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol{f}' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/74/7441a80f3132872d678c102bf8d3fdff7fc273125df73d7bd390245cd9b7bf09.svg)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,0).$ Since the point $(1,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 0, t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-2(0)+(t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is inside the interval $t \\in (-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on this boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(0,0).$\n\n- The half-circle connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2}-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives the solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in(-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of  $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain a total of four candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (0,-2), \\quad (2,0), \\quad (0,2).\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $7$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(0,-2),$  and $(0,2).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2504620-1607-5645-b4d2-e55a27da4e3f" :question/id #uuid "f2504620-1607-5645-b4d2-e55a27da4e3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37555" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}+y$ on the closed region \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}.$ \nYou are given  that $f$ has no critical points in the region and on the boundary of the region,  the points that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $(0,0)$, $(2,4),$ and  $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nNotice that we are told that $f$ has no critical points in the region.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0-2)^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,4) & =(2-2)^{2}+(4) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(1,1) & =(1-2)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f768c06e-68ed-5c8a-96fe-b36cf58285fb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f768c06e-68ed-5c8a-96fe-b36cf58285fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "f768c06e-68ed-5c8a-96fe-b36cf58285fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-57d35883-bcf4-5fb7-9e76-479830e77caf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca91afc3-4d89-510b-bc13-24a1f97adc1c" "answer-817543f2-9556-5cde-ba81-e834fd869803" "answer-a584c5a2-465a-5915-9a0f-ed12a25b03f7" "answer-52624a61-59b3-504e-b08d-3b0fb6689de1" "answer-57d35883-bcf4-5fb7-9e76-479830e77caf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca91afc3-4d89-510b-bc13-24a1f97adc1c" :answer/id #uuid "ca91afc3-4d89-510b-bc13-24a1f97adc1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-817543f2-9556-5cde-ba81-e834fd869803" :answer/id #uuid "817543f2-9556-5cde-ba81-e834fd869803" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a584c5a2-465a-5915-9a0f-ed12a25b03f7" :answer/id #uuid "a584c5a2-465a-5915-9a0f-ed12a25b03f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52624a61-59b3-504e-b08d-3b0fb6689de1" :answer/id #uuid "52624a61-59b3-504e-b08d-3b0fb6689de1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57d35883-bcf4-5fb7-9e76-479830e77caf" :answer/id #uuid "57d35883-bcf4-5fb7-9e76-479830e77caf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11bccf6f-4ced-5e74-80c9-222565fe2e6b" :question/id #uuid "11bccf6f-4ced-5e74-80c9-222565fe2e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83418" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{3}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.$ \nYou are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region: \n\n![](images/21/21edc0f327ce949027b6cfb26789442ea0f7a3be7d7c9638d540dff77062870a.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0-1)^{3}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =(1-1)^{3}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8f37801-7fba-5407-99f3-56966d38068b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8f37801-7fba-5407-99f3-56966d38068b" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f37801-7fba-5407-99f3-56966d38068b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2fcbaf68-d293-51cc-a576-3b40121223bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fcbaf68-d293-51cc-a576-3b40121223bf" "answer-49abf9f2-669a-576a-a19a-889756e18ecd" "answer-0461d323-c426-57d7-8a31-96c6ccb0b392" "answer-1b49ff3f-4e51-53f3-a015-1753de86c110" "answer-7cbe1154-dbaf-5151-a42d-da24649ac300"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fcbaf68-d293-51cc-a576-3b40121223bf" :answer/id #uuid "2fcbaf68-d293-51cc-a576-3b40121223bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49abf9f2-669a-576a-a19a-889756e18ecd" :answer/id #uuid "49abf9f2-669a-576a-a19a-889756e18ecd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0461d323-c426-57d7-8a31-96c6ccb0b392" :answer/id #uuid "0461d323-c426-57d7-8a31-96c6ccb0b392" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b49ff3f-4e51-53f3-a015-1753de86c110" :answer/id #uuid "1b49ff3f-4e51-53f3-a015-1753de86c110" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cbe1154-dbaf-5151-a42d-da24649ac300" :answer/id #uuid "7cbe1154-dbaf-5151-a42d-da24649ac300" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8bdc5f30-6e9d-55a3-aacc-08dbc13064bd" :question/id #uuid "8bdc5f30-6e9d-55a3-aacc-08dbc13064bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-167161" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(-1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-4,0)$, and $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/dc/dc5a440d527fcf7583c4a341f08f2c6b5f6b4c1dc87b75286ea0a7f569d6babe.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(-1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-4,0),\\,(4,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4,0) & =-4{e}^{-4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}} \\\\ f(4,0) & =4{e}^{4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4} \\\\ f(-1,0) & =-{e}^{-1}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-\\frac{1}{e}$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4{e}^{4}$ at the point $(4,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5bc7cdb8-c00a-5bcd-bca0-3c9f09586b74"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5bc7cdb8-c00a-5bcd-bca0-3c9f09586b74" :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc7cdb8-c00a-5bcd-bca0-3c9f09586b74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ece1eee0-81d5-54f5-aa04-0c45d7d5fd04" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d8beee5-d16b-5ad8-81c1-9b0ed36361cf" "answer-efa9e65b-368c-592e-ae82-4a598d72fc36" "answer-ece1eee0-81d5-54f5-aa04-0c45d7d5fd04" "answer-180b8d8a-e5ba-53e7-85fe-0018dfc11e0e" "answer-4d624490-8037-521f-bbf8-506c7e05ca1c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d8beee5-d16b-5ad8-81c1-9b0ed36361cf" :answer/id #uuid "3d8beee5-d16b-5ad8-81c1-9b0ed36361cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efa9e65b-368c-592e-ae82-4a598d72fc36" :answer/id #uuid "efa9e65b-368c-592e-ae82-4a598d72fc36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}={e}^{4}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ece1eee0-81d5-54f5-aa04-0c45d7d5fd04" :answer/id #uuid "ece1eee0-81d5-54f5-aa04-0c45d7d5fd04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\frac{1}{e}$ and ${f}_{max}=4{e}^{4}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-180b8d8a-e5ba-53e7-85fe-0018dfc11e0e" :answer/id #uuid "180b8d8a-e5ba-53e7-85fe-0018dfc11e0e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}$ and ${f}_{max}=4{e}^{4}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d624490-8037-521f-bbf8-506c7e05ca1c" :answer/id #uuid "4d624490-8037-521f-bbf8-506c7e05ca1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2{e}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125033 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2+6x-3y+xy$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=8.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(3,-6),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,8),$ $(1,2),$ and $(2,8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/06/06660df896e67cef560398ca24217cdba2e2b9a34fe40f1829b3e1d5d2db24e3.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,-6)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,8),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,8).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,8) & =2+6(-2)-3(8)+(-2)(8) \\\\  & =-50 \\\\ f(1,2) & =2+6(1)-3(2)+(1)(2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,8) & =2+6(2)-3(8)+(2)(8) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-50$ at the point $(-2,8),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $6$ at the point $(2,8).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122798 :answer-field/correct "answer-e08526c7-85a2-54ab-b8bb-bc02be965b47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5d065ac-c348-5dd1-a57c-c5e5b0070028" "answer-0f5adf40-0426-5a10-8817-dc97f008412e" "answer-95dff366-2d95-5d09-afa0-223b43913ab4" "answer-e08526c7-85a2-54ab-b8bb-bc02be965b47" "answer-0f08487e-af26-5c1f-962f-ed237732c2fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5d065ac-c348-5dd1-a57c-c5e5b0070028" :answer/id #uuid "f5d065ac-c348-5dd1-a57c-c5e5b0070028" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-20$ and ${f}_{max}=20$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f5adf40-0426-5a10-8817-dc97f008412e" :answer/id #uuid "0f5adf40-0426-5a10-8817-dc97f008412e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-50$ and ${f}_{max}=20$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95dff366-2d95-5d09-afa0-223b43913ab4" :answer/id #uuid "95dff366-2d95-5d09-afa0-223b43913ab4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-50$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e08526c7-85a2-54ab-b8bb-bc02be965b47" :answer/id #uuid "e08526c7-85a2-54ab-b8bb-bc02be965b47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-50$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f08487e-af26-5c1f-962f-ed237732c2fd" :answer/id #uuid "0f08487e-af26-5c1f-962f-ed237732c2fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=4$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8052460c-148b-5893-b35a-1e1c771c0935" :question/id #uuid "8052460c-148b-5893-b35a-1e1c771c0935" :question/math-academy-id "q-83423" :question/problem "Find the global minimum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe1aa21de9b6354e561eb1e82f6c83b25c71770470dc8501e6b491e35e2c391.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ is in the interior of $D$, we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(2,2),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =(1)(1)-1-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,0) & =(0)(0)-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)(0)-2-0 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,2) & =(2)(2)-2-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,2) & =(0)(2)-0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0)$and $(0,2),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da2953ed-d903-58c0-9224-6075f8a7a3be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da2953ed-d903-58c0-9224-6075f8a7a3be" :answer-field/id #uuid "da2953ed-d903-58c0-9224-6075f8a7a3be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d23eb270-d0e1-5d1c-a620-fc71aa88f9a7" "answer-3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" "answer-9e618bea-0b35-5168-96bf-90ae9dca8ae2" "answer-c070f661-5b43-5330-b698-b38f14c4686a" "answer-c137dca3-ade7-592f-92eb-70a9b9c0e301"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d23eb270-d0e1-5d1c-a620-fc71aa88f9a7" :answer/id #uuid "d23eb270-d0e1-5d1c-a620-fc71aa88f9a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" :answer/id #uuid "3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e618bea-0b35-5168-96bf-90ae9dca8ae2" :answer/id #uuid "9e618bea-0b35-5168-96bf-90ae9dca8ae2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c070f661-5b43-5330-b698-b38f14c4686a" :answer/id #uuid "c070f661-5b43-5330-b698-b38f14c4686a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c137dca3-ade7-592f-92eb-70a9b9c0e301" :answer/id #uuid "c137dca3-ade7-592f-92eb-70a9b9c0e301" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-004a7cc4-06c2-5abf-9f5d-0dfb86998289" :question/id #uuid "004a7cc4-06c2-5abf-9f5d-0dfb86998289" :question/math-academy-id "q-83434" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+2$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(1)+2 \\\\  & =2+0-4+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =2(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0+8+2 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0-8+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $18$ at the point $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c7409f8-33ba-5d76-9294-d18b4e765a0e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c7409f8-33ba-5d76-9294-d18b4e765a0e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7409f8-33ba-5d76-9294-d18b4e765a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46bc837a-bbcd-5027-bfb5-1c8984cd1027" "answer-9f15347b-2581-5183-b776-03898fbfc72e" "answer-cda725f3-ec0d-574a-ba5c-8fd6946ee6a9" "answer-a6701104-c6a3-5c18-9c13-31528c875706" "answer-601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46bc837a-bbcd-5027-bfb5-1c8984cd1027" :answer/id #uuid "46bc837a-bbcd-5027-bfb5-1c8984cd1027" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f15347b-2581-5183-b776-03898fbfc72e" :answer/id #uuid "9f15347b-2581-5183-b776-03898fbfc72e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cda725f3-ec0d-574a-ba5c-8fd6946ee6a9" :answer/id #uuid "cda725f3-ec0d-574a-ba5c-8fd6946ee6a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6701104-c6a3-5c18-9c13-31528c875706" :answer/id #uuid "a6701104-c6a3-5c18-9c13-31528c875706" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44" :answer/id #uuid "601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=18$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e78c82da-b48f-5dab-aeb6-fcde924129f1" :question/id #uuid "e78c82da-b48f-5dab-aeb6-fcde924129f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83438" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=3xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3y,\\,3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3y=0 \\\\ 3x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(1,-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5fc95411-56c6-5cbd-8b43-384ac78b9b38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5fc95411-56c6-5cbd-8b43-384ac78b9b38" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc95411-56c6-5cbd-8b43-384ac78b9b38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b1ed88c-33aa-5e4b-93e8-e563837660ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0e3e8dda-0412-5eec-8ede-228822513c7f" "answer-8b1ed88c-33aa-5e4b-93e8-e563837660ea" "answer-8b7b1804-b1be-5c7a-9e65-f3a8f8ab47ab" "answer-147e44af-3fa3-5f20-8f78-baf96a687c49" "answer-f73f6628-1e5d-56c5-9576-c62c5389fe91"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e3e8dda-0412-5eec-8ede-228822513c7f" :answer/id #uuid "0e3e8dda-0412-5eec-8ede-228822513c7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b1ed88c-33aa-5e4b-93e8-e563837660ea" :answer/id #uuid "8b1ed88c-33aa-5e4b-93e8-e563837660ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b7b1804-b1be-5c7a-9e65-f3a8f8ab47ab" :answer/id #uuid "8b7b1804-b1be-5c7a-9e65-f3a8f8ab47ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-147e44af-3fa3-5f20-8f78-baf96a687c49" :answer/id #uuid "147e44af-3fa3-5f20-8f78-baf96a687c49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f73f6628-1e5d-56c5-9576-c62c5389fe91" :answer/id #uuid "f73f6628-1e5d-56c5-9576-c62c5389fe91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" :question/id #uuid "a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" :question/math-academy-id "q-83437" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has no critical points." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81180c93-e1c2-5e9f-adf8-f0c58db4c467"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81180c93-e1c2-5e9f-adf8-f0c58db4c467" :answer-field/id #uuid "81180c93-e1c2-5e9f-adf8-f0c58db4c467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" "answer-a52483f7-a6b6-5cc1-abb3-7db0b579ef56" "answer-ab7ee3bb-67c1-5270-8d84-3e8e37bf67a9" "answer-efcb8428-944f-5ad5-960c-f08b95cb6ba3" "answer-b3a76143-6f93-57f5-9713-8433062ded47"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" :answer/id #uuid "70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a52483f7-a6b6-5cc1-abb3-7db0b579ef56" :answer/id #uuid "a52483f7-a6b6-5cc1-abb3-7db0b579ef56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab7ee3bb-67c1-5270-8d84-3e8e37bf67a9" :answer/id #uuid "ab7ee3bb-67c1-5270-8d84-3e8e37bf67a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efcb8428-944f-5ad5-960c-f08b95cb6ba3" :answer/id #uuid "efcb8428-944f-5ad5-960c-f08b95cb6ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3a76143-6f93-57f5-9713-8433062ded47" :answer/id #uuid "b3a76143-6f93-57f5-9713-8433062ded47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-49397200-f106-5784-934d-a685f0e1e2bc" :question/id #uuid "49397200-f106-5784-934d-a685f0e1e2bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83436" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4xy+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 8\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y,\\,2y-4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y=0 \\\\ 2y-4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+1 \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t+8{sin}^{2}⁡t-32\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -32\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{8},0),\\,(2,2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(-2,-2),\\,(2,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{8},0)$ | $(2,2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(-2,-2)$ | $(2,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ | $-7$ | $25$ | $-7$ | $25$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e141f0c3-e7b5-5bab-8504-cfef40829a43"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e141f0c3-e7b5-5bab-8504-cfef40829a43" :answer-field/id #uuid "e141f0c3-e7b5-5bab-8504-cfef40829a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4873054d-389b-5657-b900-41aaf6b8506c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90e36d8d-b6c9-504e-9e35-d0e7572f1045" "answer-4873054d-389b-5657-b900-41aaf6b8506c" "answer-daf4365c-6130-56d7-9fa9-feb169811efd" "answer-d431810c-ed86-5648-8955-de7e2061891b" "answer-8ee71f7a-8717-5e5a-914c-2dd0b5abd661"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90e36d8d-b6c9-504e-9e35-d0e7572f1045" :answer/id #uuid "90e36d8d-b6c9-504e-9e35-d0e7572f1045" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4873054d-389b-5657-b900-41aaf6b8506c" :answer/id #uuid "4873054d-389b-5657-b900-41aaf6b8506c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and  ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daf4365c-6130-56d7-9fa9-feb169811efd" :answer/id #uuid "daf4365c-6130-56d7-9fa9-feb169811efd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d431810c-ed86-5648-8955-de7e2061891b" :answer/id #uuid "d431810c-ed86-5648-8955-de7e2061891b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=9$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ee71f7a-8717-5e5a-914c-2dd0b5abd661" :answer/id #uuid "8ee71f7a-8717-5e5a-914c-2dd0b5abd661" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=9$ and  ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8fb41ea9-db84-5bfb-a8a6-5486c5782ac8" :question/id #uuid "8fb41ea9-db84-5bfb-a8a6-5486c5782ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86720" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=0 \\\\ -2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+3 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(2,0)$ and $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c12a56a-e777-5adf-89ed-09fc3bb0be46"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c12a56a-e777-5adf-89ed-09fc3bb0be46" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c12a56a-e777-5adf-89ed-09fc3bb0be46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7f6f3732-88ff-52f3-b96e-e2ff73cb9542" :answer-field/choices ["answer-001a4990-bc6c-5661-a5d2-d58cf6282115" "answer-b3c3cddb-38d0-5a99-aa10-c92fe4168383" "answer-7f6f3732-88ff-52f3-b96e-e2ff73cb9542" "answer-833fa5da-99e1-5b84-94da-cea7de15a066" "answer-836e3ef0-8c12-55a7-9852-b91d4df5f503"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-001a4990-bc6c-5661-a5d2-d58cf6282115" :answer/id #uuid "001a4990-bc6c-5661-a5d2-d58cf6282115" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3c3cddb-38d0-5a99-aa10-c92fe4168383" :answer/id #uuid "b3c3cddb-38d0-5a99-aa10-c92fe4168383" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f6f3732-88ff-52f3-b96e-e2ff73cb9542" :answer/id #uuid "7f6f3732-88ff-52f3-b96e-e2ff73cb9542" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-833fa5da-99e1-5b84-94da-cea7de15a066" :answer/id #uuid "833fa5da-99e1-5b84-94da-cea7de15a066" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-836e3ef0-8c12-55a7-9852-b91d4df5f503" :answer/id #uuid "836e3ef0-8c12-55a7-9852-b91d4df5f503" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=3$ and  ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186373325 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0331b5d4c9fa3c823d6596c1ab147b8ee459feb4a4feaed3c614e2639ae77e5.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-xy$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-y,\\,-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4-y=0 \\\\ -x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,4).$ Since the point $(0,4)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(0) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=0,$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(0,4).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,4),\\,(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $8$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $8$ at the points $(2,0).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-57601ebb-8cf3-5513-9b66-f85999e2eebc" :question/id #uuid "57601ebb-8cf3-5513-9b66-f85999e2eebc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86707" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+4x$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(-2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2y+4,\\,2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+4=0 \\\\ 2x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the point $(0,-2).$ Since $(0,-2)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema. It lies on the boundary of $D,$ which we consider in the next step.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =2(t)(0)+4(t) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,-4)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-4,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =2(0)(t)+4(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (-4,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-2,0⟩+t(⟨0,-4⟩-⟨-2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨-2,0⟩+t⟨2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2+2t,-4t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(-2+2t,-4t) \\\\  & =2(-2+2t)(-4t)+4(-2+2t) \\\\  & =-16{t}^{2}+24t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-16{t}^{2}+24t-8)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -32t+24 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(-\\frac{1}{2},-3).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,-4),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-3)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e76e0c74-60a1-5961-a65f-5e2fb44fef4f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e76e0c74-60a1-5961-a65f-5e2fb44fef4f" :answer-field/id #uuid "e76e0c74-60a1-5961-a65f-5e2fb44fef4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4051cf24-382c-5bd2-aff3-583e10956692" :answer-field/choices ["answer-74ceb128-bbd0-50cf-8cea-9b2c8526afbb" "answer-fbf1ced2-6bee-5963-ade0-b42da6d0f467" "answer-6142a979-2e6c-514e-bb2f-28e05f4b2443" "answer-4051cf24-382c-5bd2-aff3-583e10956692" "answer-71d5c619-fc88-599e-8d90-28ac4a12393d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74ceb128-bbd0-50cf-8cea-9b2c8526afbb" :answer/id #uuid "74ceb128-bbd0-50cf-8cea-9b2c8526afbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbf1ced2-6bee-5963-ade0-b42da6d0f467" :answer/id #uuid "fbf1ced2-6bee-5963-ade0-b42da6d0f467" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6142a979-2e6c-514e-bb2f-28e05f4b2443" :answer/id #uuid "6142a979-2e6c-514e-bb2f-28e05f4b2443" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4051cf24-382c-5bd2-aff3-583e10956692" :answer/id #uuid "4051cf24-382c-5bd2-aff3-583e10956692" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71d5c619-fc88-599e-8d90-28ac4a12393d" :answer/id #uuid "71d5c619-fc88-599e-8d90-28ac4a12393d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22939b6a-86a1-5a24-b491-90aeb5c6c381" :question/id #uuid "22939b6a-86a1-5a24-b491-90aeb5c6c381" :question/math-academy-id "q-83475" :question/problem "![](images/85/856b131ba85b8fd1da0050ead917d5757b4b4eea46aca54bef7d7b103a896bc6.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\le 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve  ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=0 \\\\ -2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-(t)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2{t}^{2}-3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 4t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution, $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain three candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the points $(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $5$ at the point $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50157cd0-2717-583c-a92a-90b79b4a9208"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50157cd0-2717-583c-a92a-90b79b4a9208" :answer-field/id #uuid "50157cd0-2717-583c-a92a-90b79b4a9208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dee9ce0f-f146-51ac-9454-2608b257ca8a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-323833f4-28f9-5c1a-bbca-309065f42ade" "answer-a188385c-2f9e-51b4-a7fa-d5e0f807d0eb" "answer-893491fd-56a8-5aa8-832d-6349db8bebd9" "answer-ef4964f5-c0a5-552c-bdc7-21ec7c82e1fd" "answer-dee9ce0f-f146-51ac-9454-2608b257ca8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-323833f4-28f9-5c1a-bbca-309065f42ade" :answer/id #uuid "323833f4-28f9-5c1a-bbca-309065f42ade" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a188385c-2f9e-51b4-a7fa-d5e0f807d0eb" :answer/id #uuid "a188385c-2f9e-51b4-a7fa-d5e0f807d0eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-893491fd-56a8-5aa8-832d-6349db8bebd9" :answer/id #uuid "893491fd-56a8-5aa8-832d-6349db8bebd9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef4964f5-c0a5-552c-bdc7-21ec7c82e1fd" :answer/id #uuid "ef4964f5-c0a5-552c-bdc7-21ec7c82e1fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dee9ce0f-f146-51ac-9454-2608b257ca8a" :answer/id #uuid "dee9ce0f-f146-51ac-9454-2608b257ca8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e240afca-8d54-5fc1-a704-b3948ecfdb13" :question/id #uuid "e240afca-8d54-5fc1-a704-b3948ecfdb13" :question/math-academy-id "q-86706" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9ce1b06ac9fc3caeed0a1f7866511be42b4c127a0bdddfd40b74e211ed24e02a.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2},\\,4xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4xy=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=R \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the entire segment of solutions $\\{(x,0)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2]\\}.$ Since all these points do not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include them in the list of candidates for extrema (they lie on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider them in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =2(t)(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =2(0)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(0,2)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2(t)(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2} \\\\  & =8t-2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (8t-2{t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 8-6{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(4-3{t}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ inside the interval $t\\in (0,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one  candidate point, $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the points on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ at the point $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3977c16f-2769-5840-b0e0-c58d7f40d24e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3977c16f-2769-5840-b0e0-c58d7f40d24e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3977c16f-2769-5840-b0e0-c58d7f40d24e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cc7bc595-ca68-55de-b3f4-a09683d61d2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83e904a1-7bfa-5bb4-900c-0134ca087247" "answer-955410e1-ed87-5d07-bed0-cc97700dd886" "answer-cc7bc595-ca68-55de-b3f4-a09683d61d2b" "answer-46ef9306-79e8-5995-bcb9-402ef07b1eef" "answer-1d1b623e-7729-5df0-a681-2ee123f5e5bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83e904a1-7bfa-5bb4-900c-0134ca087247" :answer/id #uuid "83e904a1-7bfa-5bb4-900c-0134ca087247" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-955410e1-ed87-5d07-bed0-cc97700dd886" :answer/id #uuid "955410e1-ed87-5d07-bed0-cc97700dd886" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc7bc595-ca68-55de-b3f4-a09683d61d2b" :answer/id #uuid "cc7bc595-ca68-55de-b3f4-a09683d61d2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46ef9306-79e8-5995-bcb9-402ef07b1eef" :answer/id #uuid "46ef9306-79e8-5995-bcb9-402ef07b1eef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=\\frac{32}{9}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d1b623e-7729-5df0-a681-2ee123f5e5bc" :answer/id #uuid "1d1b623e-7729-5df0-a681-2ee123f5e5bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=32\\sqrt{3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284579 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-y & 1-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1-y=0 \\\\ 1-x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =t+0-t(0) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t⟨1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,t) \\\\  & =t+t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2t-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2-2t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t=1$ does not belong to the range $t\\in (0,1),$ we do not include the corresponding point in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =1+t-t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total, we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125892 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/b4/b48de1dcd3a240a1f1904dd290ef0665fc603c4cc5f99915915c6a12f79d74d9.svg)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $e$ | $1/e$ | $e$ | $1/e$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124737 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
